  "difficulty": "billable_bird",
  "industry": "Airlines",
  "intro": "The client is a legacy carrier operating a major transatlantic route between New York and London. Load factors have been strong, and the route team is evaluating a modest fare increase to capture additional margin before the summer travel season. The question is whether volume erosion will offset the per-ticket uplift.",
  "facts": [
    "Current average fare on the route is $270 per ticket",
    "Annual passenger volume is 47,500 travelers",
    "Variable cost per passenger (fuel, catering, fees) is $18",
    "Fixed costs for operating this route are $110,000 annually",
    "Price elasticity of demand on this route is estimated at -0.6",
    "Proposed fare increase is 5%"
  ],
  "question": "What is the profit impact in dollars of implementing the 5% fare increase?",
  "guiding_nudge": "Calculate the new price and new volume separately, then compare total profit before and after the increase.",
  "framework_soundbite": "Price up means margin per unit rises but volume falls — the question is always which effect is bigger.",
  "worked_solution": [
    {
      "calc": "$270 × (1 + 0.05)",
      "step": 1,
      "label": "New price",
      "result": 283.5
    },
    {
      "calc": "47500 × (-0.6 × 0.05)",
      "step": 2,
      "label": "Volume change",
      "result": -1425
    },
    {
      "calc": "47500 + -1425",
      "step": 3,
      "label": "New volume",
      "result": 46075
    },
    {
      "calc": "($270 − $18) × 47500 − $110000",
      "step": 4,
      "label": "Current profit",
      "result": 11860000
    },
    {
      "calc": "($283.5 − $18) × 46075 − $110000",
      "step": 5,
      "label": "New profit",
      "result": 12122913
    },
    {
      "calc": "$12122913 − $11860000",
      "step": 6,
      "label": "Profit delta",
      "result": 262913
    }
  ],
  "final_answer": 262913,
  "business_interpretation": "A profit gain of approximately $263K suggests the route has pricing power and the increase should proceed before competitive response erodes the opportunity.",
